Q: Defining Function Types in C A: <p>In this reply, I am assuming that the data type of the two function arguments and of the return value are always the same. For example, I am assuming that if the first function arguments is of type <code>double</code>, then the other argument and the return value is also of type <code>double</code>.</p>
<p>In C, the best you can do is</p>
<ol>
<li>to pass a data structure to the function that is able to represent all possible data types that you want, and to return such a data structure, or</li>
<li>to define one function for each of the possible types that you want to use, and then, when calling the function, use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/c/language/generic" rel="nofollow noreferrer">generic selection</a> so that the appropriate function for the type is automatically called.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is a demonstration program for #1:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

enum
{
    DATA_TYPE_INT,
    DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE,

    // this value must be last in the list
    DATA_TYPE_INVALID
};

typedef struct
{
    unsigned char type;
    union
    {
        int i;
        double d;
    };

} any_type;

// This function will create a value of type &quot;any_type&quot; from an &quot;int&quot;.
any_type create_int( int value )
{
    return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_INT, .i = value };
}

// This function will create a value of type &quot;any_type&quot; from a &quot;double&quot;.
any_type create_double( double value )
{
    return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE, .d = value };
}

// This function will halve the value passed to it.
any_type halve_value( any_type value )
{
    switch ( value.type )
    {
        case DATA_TYPE_INT:
            value.i /= 2;
            break;

        case DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE:
            value.d /= 2;
            break;

        default:
            printf( &quot;WARNING: halve operation failed due to invalid data type!\n&quot; );
            return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_INVALID };
    }

    return value;
}

void print_value( any_type value )
{
    switch ( value.type )
    {
        case DATA_TYPE_INT:
            printf
            (
                &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
                &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
                value.i
            );
            break;

        case DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE:
            printf
            (
                &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
                &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
                value.d
            );
            break;

        default:
            printf( &quot;The type is INVALID.\n&quot; );
    }
}

int main( void )
{
    any_type i = create_int( 123 );
    any_type d = create_double( 456.78 );

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the following output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:

First value:
The type is &quot;int&quot;.
The value is: 123

Second value:
The type is &quot;double&quot;.
The value is: 456.780000


VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:

First value:
The type is &quot;int&quot;.
The value is: 61

Second value:
The type is &quot;double&quot;.
The value is: 228.390000
</code></pre>
<p>Here is a demonstration program for #2:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define halve_value( X ) \
    _Generic \
    ( \
        ( X ), \
        int:     halve_value_int, \
        double:  halve_value_double \
    ) \
    ( X )

#define print_value( X ) \
    _Generic \
    ( \
        ( X ), \
        int:     print_value_int, \
        double:  print_value_double \
    ) \
    ( X )

int halve_value_int( int value )
{
    return value /= 2;
}

double halve_value_double( double value )
{
    return value /= 2;
}

void print_value_int( int value )
{
    printf
    (
        &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
        &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
        value
    );
}

void print_value_double( double value )
{
    printf
    (
        &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
        &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
        value
    );
}

int main( void )
{
    int i    = 123;
    double d = 456.78;

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This demonstration program has the same output as the first one.</p>
<p>However, if you want a C-like programming language with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_overloading" rel="nofollow noreferrer">function overloading</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_(C%2B%2B)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">templates</a> built into the core language, then I recommend that you take a look at the programming language C++. That language allows you to use the same code to write the function <code>halve_value</code> once for all possible data types, assuming that you want to use the <code>/</code> operator to divide by <code>2</code> for all types, and it automatically creates the code for the functions for all of the different types for you. This allows you to rewrite the demonstration programs above the following way:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

template &lt;typename T&gt;
T halve_value( T value )
{
    return value / 2;
}

template &lt;typename T&gt;
void print_value( T value )
{
    if ( std::is_same&lt;T, int&gt;::value )
    {
        printf
        (
            &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
            &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
            value
        );
    }    
    else if ( std::is_same&lt;T, double&gt;::value )
    {
        printf
        (
            &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
            &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
            value
        );
    }
    else
    {
        printf( &quot;The type is not int or double.\n&quot; );
    }
}

int main()
{
    int i    = 123;
    double d = 456.78;

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program also has the same output as the other two demonstration programs.</p>
<p>Note that most C++ programmers would not use the function <code>printf</code>, as that is considered bad style in C++. They would rather use a function that is part of the C++ standard library, which allows you to print in a type-generic way. However, for consistency with the other two examples which are written in C, I also used the function <code>printf</code> in the C++ demonstration program.</p>
